{
  "requirement": "For node reads, the SDK constructs a `Snapshot` from\nthe contents returned by `Domain::node_content` and their\n`NodeContents::fingerprint` under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint`, without another read.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct Snapshot<T> {\n    pub content: T,\n    pub fingerprint: Fingerprint,\n}\n\nimpl<T> Snapshot<T> {\n    pub fn new(content: T, fingerprint: Fingerprint) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<T: Clone> Clone for Snapshot<T>;\nimpl<T: Debug> Debug for Snapshot<T>;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.graph.snapshot`. The one weak spot is that nothing exercises the `Clone` and `Debug` impls, which I judged exempt.\n\n**Obligations and the tests that cover them**\n\n1. **A node read builds a `Snapshot` from the `Domain::node_content` result and its `NodeContents::fingerprint`.**\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions]` asserts that `read_snapshot(&1).fingerprint` equals `fp(\"before\")`. It also asserts that the recorded dependencies keep the distinct \"before\" and \"after\" fingerprints.\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[direct_reads_return_complete_unicode_contents_and_snapshots]` checks both an empty string and a large multi-byte string. It asserts that `snapshot.content.format()` equals the original and `snapshot.fingerprint` equals `fp(original)`. It also asserts that the recorded dependency carries the same fingerprint.\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot]` asserts that `snapshot.content.fingerprint()` equals `snapshot.fingerprint`. It also asserts that `snapshot.fingerprint.value` is `\"before\"`.\n   - `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk]` checks, for a second domain, that retained contents keep their fingerprint after the underlying files change. It also checks that `node_content` fingerprints match the retained ones.\n   - The agent tests `graph_tools_record_delivered_snapshots_and_prepared_pages_do_not_record_delivery` and `agent_edge_tools_record_selections_without_validating_targets` check that snapshots feed the recorded dependencies, and the first of them checks the fingerprint of a node read.\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[explicit_insertions_preserve_order_repetition_and_selection_snapshots]` asserts the recorded `Dependency::Node` fingerprints for contents read through `ask`.\n\n2. **\"Without another read.\"**\n   - `snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions` asserts the exact read log `[1, 1, 1, 2]`. That is one `node_content` call per `read` or `read_snapshot` call, including the failing read of node 2.\n   - `direct_reads_return_complete_unicode_contents_and_snapshots` asserts reads `[1, 1]` for one `read_snapshot` plus one `read`.\n   - `identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot` asserts reads `[1]` after the underlying content changes. It also asserts that the snapshot's fingerprint and content stay \"before\" and that `content.fingerprint()` still matches the stored fingerprint.\n\n3. **Public struct shape and `new`.**\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[callers_can_pair_content_with_its_fingerprint]` runs from an external crate. It calls `Snapshot::new(content, fingerprint)` and asserts the public `content` and `fingerprint` fields.\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks that the export is present.\n\n4. **`#[non_exhaustive]`, `Clone` and `Debug`.**\n   - No test clones or debug-formats a `Snapshot`, and none tests `#[non_exhaustive]` through a compile-fail check.\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs#struct[Snapshot]` implements these with `#[derive(Clone, Debug)]` and the `#[non_exhaustive]` attribute. These are declarative and compile-time. They have no conditionals or transformations of their own, so I treated them as exempt from needing a test.\n\n**Cited requirement.** `requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint` is context only. The snapshot tests do apply it, because they compare the snapshot fingerprint with the contents' own `fingerprint()`.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none, and no `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
